# BIOMECHANICS

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Biomechanics model describes how forces act on the human body and how the body produces, absorbs and transfers those forces during movement.

It provides the mechanical foundation required to understand exercise technique, athletic performance and combat-specific actions.

Biomechanics does not determine training decisions by itself.

It provides knowledge that allows CAS to make those decisions more accurately.

---

# Definition

Biomechanics is the study of movement and the forces that produce or modify movement.

CAS uses biomechanics to understand:

- how movement is produced;
- where force originates;
- how force is transmitted;
- how joints and tissues are loaded;
- why one exercise differs mechanically from another;
- how physical preparation may transfer to combat performance.

---

# Core Principle

Movement is the result of force interacting with the body.

Every exercise and every combat action can be described through:

Force.

Direction.

Magnitude.

Velocity.

Acceleration.

Position.

Time.

The quality of movement depends not only on how much force is produced,

but on where,

when,

and in which direction it is applied.

---

# Scientific Role

The Biomechanics model informs:

Exercise Ontology.

Exercise Classification.

Movement Patterns.

Capability Mapping.

Strength.

Power.

Speed.

Mobility.

Stability.

Safety Engine.

Exercise Knowledge Engine.

Session Generation Engine.

Explainability Engine.

---

# Internal and External Forces

CAS distinguishes between external and internal forces.

External Forces

Examples:

Gravity.

Ground reaction force.

Opponent resistance.

Equipment resistance.

Impact.

Friction.

Air resistance.

Internal Forces

Examples:

Muscle force.

Tendon force.

Ligament tension.

Joint reaction force.

Bone compression.

Training decisions should consider both.

---

# Force

Force can:

Accelerate the body.

Decelerate the body.

Change direction.

Stabilize a position.

Deform tissue.

Produce impact.

Force must always be interpreted with direction and context.

---

# Force Vectors

Every force has:

Magnitude.

Direction.

Point of application.

An exercise becomes mechanically specific partly because of its force vector.

Examples:

Vertical force.

Horizontal force.

Rotational force.

Lateral force.

Diagonal force.

Combat performance requires force production in multiple directions.

---

# Ground Reaction Force

When the athlete applies force into the ground,

the ground applies an equal and opposite force to the athlete.

Ground reaction force contributes to:

Sprinting.

Jumping.

Punching.

Kicking.

Throwing.

Level changes.

Footwork.

Effective combat movement begins with interaction with the ground.

---

# Kinetic Chain

Force often travels through a sequence of body segments.

Example:

Ground

↓

Foot

↓

Leg

↓

Hip

↓

Trunk

↓

Shoulder

↓

Arm

↓

Fist

An inefficient link reduces total force transfer.

CAS should evaluate movements as coordinated chains,

not isolated muscles.

---

# Proximal-to-Distal Sequencing

Many explosive actions follow a proximal-to-distal sequence.

Larger body segments initiate movement.

Smaller distal segments complete it.

Examples:

Punching.

Kicking.

Throwing.

Medicine ball throws.

Poor timing between segments reduces speed and impact.

---

# Linear Motion

Linear motion occurs when the body or an object moves along a path.

Descriptors include:

Position.

Distance.

Displacement.

Velocity.

Acceleration.

Examples:

Sprint.

Sled push.

Linear punch.

Forward level change.

---

# Angular Motion

Angular motion occurs around an axis.

Descriptors include:

Angular displacement.

Angular velocity.

Angular acceleration.

Torque.

Examples:

Hip rotation.

Shoulder rotation.

Roundhouse kick.

Hook punch.

Throwing.

Most combat actions combine linear and angular motion.

---

# Torque

Torque is the rotational effect of force around a joint or axis.

Torque depends on:

Force magnitude.

Moment arm.

Angle of force application.

Joint position.

Higher external torque generally requires greater internal muscular torque.

---

# Moment Arm

The moment arm is the perpendicular distance between the force line and the axis of rotation.

Changes in joint position alter moment arms.

This explains why an exercise may feel harder at specific joint angles.

Exercise difficulty is not determined by load alone.

---

# Levers

The human body operates through lever systems.

Bones act as levers.

Joints act as axes.

Muscles produce force.

Most human movement favors speed and range of motion rather than mechanical efficiency.

This has important consequences for training and injury risk.

---

# Work

Mechanical work occurs when force produces displacement.

Work depends on:

Force.

Displacement.

Direction.

Training volume may approximate work,

but volume alone does not fully describe mechanical demand.

---

# Power

Mechanical power describes how rapidly work is performed.

Power depends on both force and velocity.

Biomechanics connects:

Maximum Strength.

Movement Velocity.

Rate of Force Development.

Explosive Performance.

---

# Impulse

Impulse is force applied over time.

Impulse influences changes in momentum.

A high force applied too briefly may produce less total effect than a lower force applied for longer.

Combat actions require context-specific impulse.

Examples:

Punch impact.

Takedown entry.

Jump takeoff.

Blocking.

---

# Momentum

Momentum depends on mass and velocity.

In combat,

momentum influences:

Impact.

Body collision.

Takedowns.

Throws.

Movement control.

Effective athletes manipulate both their own momentum and that of the opponent.

---

# Impact

Impact occurs when force is applied over a very short duration.

Impact characteristics depend on:

Velocity.

Effective mass.

Contact area.

Tissue stiffness.

Alignment.

Timing.

Impact performance should not be reduced to muscular strength alone.

---

# Effective Mass

Effective mass describes how much of the athlete's body mass contributes meaningfully to an impact.

Good kinetic-chain organization increases effective mass.

Poor coordination causes force leakage.

This is particularly relevant to:

Punches.

Kicks.

Elbows.

Knees.

Shoulder strikes.

---

# Force Absorption

Combat athletes must produce and absorb force.

Force absorption depends on:

Eccentric strength.

Joint alignment.

Muscle activation.

Movement strategy.

Surface.

Contact time.

Examples:

Landing.

Blocking.

Receiving contact.

Decelerating.

Sprawling.

---

# Deceleration

Deceleration is an active physical capacity.

It requires:

Eccentric force.

Coordination.

Stability.

Anticipation.

Deceleration supports:

Direction changes.

Defensive movement.

Strike recovery.

Landing.

Injury prevention.

---

# Center of Mass

The center of mass influences:

Balance.

Stability.

Acceleration.

Direction changes.

Takedowns.

Throws.

Lowering or shifting the center of mass changes mechanical possibilities.

---

# Base of Support

The base of support is the area beneath the athlete that supports body position.

A wider base generally improves stability.

A narrower base may improve mobility.

Combat requires continuous adjustment between these demands.

---

# Balance and Postural Control

Postural control depends on the relationship between:

Center of mass.

Base of support.

External forces.

Sensory information.

Muscular response.

Static balance alone does not represent combat stability.

---

# Joint Actions

Every exercise should identify relevant joint actions.

Examples:

Flexion.

Extension.

Abduction.

Adduction.

Internal rotation.

External rotation.

Pronation.

Supination.

Plantar flexion.

Dorsiflexion.

Joint actions should be described in functional context.

---

# Planes of Motion

CAS recognizes three primary anatomical planes.

Sagittal Plane

Flexion and extension dominant.

Frontal Plane

Lateral movement dominant.

Transverse Plane

Rotation dominant.

Combat actions are usually multiplanar.

Training should not remain exclusively sagittal.

---

# Axes of Rotation

Movement occurs around axes.

Frontal Axis.

Sagittal Axis.

Longitudinal Axis.

Understanding axes helps describe rotation and joint loading.

---

# Range of Motion

Range of motion influences:

Mechanical work.

Muscle length.

Joint torque.

Technical demands.

Adaptation.

More range is not automatically better.

The optimal range depends on:

Objective.

Athlete mobility.

Safety.

Sport transfer.

Exercise variation.

---

# Open and Closed Kinetic Chains

Open Kinetic Chain

The distal segment moves freely.

Examples:

Leg extension.

Open-chain kicking drill.

Closed Kinetic Chain

The distal segment is fixed or supported.

Examples:

Squat.

Push-up.

Most exercises exist on a continuum rather than in absolute categories.

---

# Bilateral and Unilateral Movement

Bilateral movement uses both sides together.

Unilateral movement emphasizes one side.

Combat athletes require both.

Unilateral training may improve:

Balance.

Coordination.

Force asymmetry.

Sport-specific stance control.

Bilateral training may allow greater absolute loading.

---

# Concentric Actions

Concentric muscle action occurs when muscle force produces shortening.

Examples:

Rising from a squat.

Accelerating a punch.

Jump takeoff.

---

# Eccentric Actions

Eccentric muscle action occurs when muscle force resists lengthening.

Examples:

Landing.

Deceleration.

Strike recovery.

Lowering a load.

Eccentric capacity is essential for force absorption.

---

# Isometric Actions

Isometric muscle action produces force without visible joint movement.

Examples:

Clinch control.

Grip fighting.

Maintaining guard.

Positional control.

Isometric strength is joint-angle specific.

---

# Stretch-Shortening Cycle

The stretch-shortening cycle combines:

Rapid eccentric loading.

Brief transition.

Concentric action.

It contributes to:

Jumping.

Sprinting.

Reactive footwork.

Punching.

Kicking.

Effective use depends on stiffness, timing and coordination.

---

# Muscle-Tendon Unit

Muscles and tendons work together.

Muscles produce active force.

Tendons transmit and store elastic energy.

The mechanical behavior of the muscle-tendon unit influences:

Power.

Efficiency.

Reactive strength.

Movement economy.

---

# Force-Length Relationship

Muscle force capacity changes with muscle length.

Very shortened or excessively lengthened positions may reduce active force production.

Exercise selection and range of motion influence this relationship.

---

# Force-Velocity Relationship

Concentric force capacity generally decreases as movement velocity increases.

Eccentric force capacity behaves differently.

This relationship informs:

Strength training.

Power training.

Velocity-specific exercise selection.

Load prescription.

---

# Length-Tension and Joint Position

Joint position changes:

Muscle length.

Moment arm.

Leverage.

Force potential.

Exercise variations should therefore not be treated as mechanically identical.

---

# Mechanical Specificity

Transfer improves when training shares important mechanical features with performance.

Relevant features include:

Force direction.

Velocity.

Range of motion.

Contraction type.

Movement sequence.

Posture.

Time available for force production.

Mechanical similarity alone does not guarantee transfer,

but it informs exercise selection.

---

# Biomechanical Transfer to Combat

CAS evaluates transfer through:

Movement pattern similarity.

Force-vector similarity.

Velocity similarity.

Contraction similarity.

Kinetic-chain similarity.

Postural similarity.

Temporal similarity.

Transfer should be justified,

not assumed.

---

# Striking Biomechanics

Effective striking may depend on:

Ground reaction force.

Lower-body drive.

Hip rotation.

Trunk rotation.

Scapular control.

Segment sequencing.

Effective mass.

Rapid limb recovery.

Different strikes use different mechanical strategies.

---

# Kicking Biomechanics

Kicking may depend on:

Support-leg stability.

Hip mobility.

Pelvic rotation.

Angular velocity.

Segment sequencing.

Balance.

Rapid retraction.

Impact mechanics.

---

# Grappling Biomechanics

Grappling may depend on:

Base of support.

Center-of-mass manipulation.

Leverage.

Grip.

Isometric force.

Rotational force.

Friction.

Opponent momentum.

Relative position often matters more than absolute strength.

---

# Throwing Biomechanics

Throws involve:

Off-balancing.

Position.

Leverage.

Rotation.

Force direction.

Timing.

Momentum manipulation.

Physical preparation should support these mechanisms without pretending to replace technical practice.

---

# Footwork Biomechanics

Footwork involves:

Force application.

Deceleration.

Reacceleration.

Center-of-mass control.

Stance width.

Ground contact.

Postural recovery.

Efficient footwork minimizes unnecessary motion.

---

# Exercise Comparison

CAS compares exercises mechanically.

Example:

Back Squat

and

Belt Squat

Both train the Squat pattern.

However,

they differ in:

Axial loading.

Trunk demand.

Equipment.

Stability.

Fatigue profile.

Exercise substitution must preserve the required adaptation,

not merely the movement name.

---

# Mechanical Load

Mechanical load includes more than external weight.

It may involve:

Joint torque.

Impact.

Velocity.

Range of motion.

Eccentric demand.

Time under tension.

Stability requirement.

External load alone is an incomplete descriptor.

---

# Tissue Stress

Different tissues respond differently to loading.

Muscle.

Tendon.

Ligament.

Bone.

Cartilage.

Exercise selection should consider both adaptation and tissue stress.

---

# Technique

Technique is the movement strategy used to solve a task.

There may be more than one acceptable strategy.

CAS should distinguish:

Safe variation.

Individual variation.

Performance variation.

Technical error.

Technique should not be judged solely by visual conformity.

---

# Individual Morphology

Biomechanics varies with:

Limb lengths.

Joint structure.

Body proportions.

Mass distribution.

Mobility.

Previous injury.

A mechanically appropriate exercise for one athlete may be inappropriate for another.

---

# Asymmetry

Human movement is not perfectly symmetrical.

CAS distinguishes:

Normal functional asymmetry.

Sport-specific asymmetry.

Potentially limiting asymmetry.

Painful asymmetry.

Asymmetry alone should not automatically trigger correction.

---

# Fatigue and Biomechanics

Fatigue may alter:

Joint position.

Force direction.

Movement velocity.

Coordination.

Range of motion.

Stability.

Technical breakdown is often a biomechanical expression of fatigue.

---

# Safety

Biomechanical analysis supports safety by identifying:

High joint torque.

Poor force distribution.

Uncontrolled deceleration.

Inadequate stability.

Excessive range.

Unsafe equipment interaction.

Biomechanics informs risk.

It does not predict injury with certainty.

---

# Assessment

Possible biomechanical assessment includes:

Video analysis.

Force plates.

Velocity measurement.

Jump testing.

Movement observation.

Range-of-motion testing.

Balance testing.

Wearable sensors.

Assessment methods should match the decision being made.

---

# Data Quality

Biomechanical conclusions depend on measurement quality.

CAS distinguishes:

Directly measured.

Reliably estimated.

Subjectively observed.

Unknown.

Low-quality data should reduce confidence.

---

# Explainability

Biomechanical reasoning should remain understandable.

Example:

The landmine press was selected because its diagonal force direction and reduced overhead mobility requirement better match your current shoulder capacity.

Avoid unnecessary technical jargon in athlete-facing explanations.

