# Scientific Foundation

The model integrates knowledge from:

Mechanics.

Anatomy.

Kinesiology.

Motor Control.

Strength and Conditioning.

Sports Medicine.

Combat Sports Science.

Scientific uncertainty should always be acknowledged.

---

# Limitations

Biomechanics describes mechanical relationships.

It cannot fully explain:

Tactical intelligence.

Psychological state.

Skill.

Pain.

Adaptation.

Competition outcome.

Mechanical efficiency is only one component of performance.

---

# Future Expansion

Future versions may include:

Computer vision.

Three-dimensional motion capture.

Force-plate integration.

Wearable inertial sensors.

Strike velocity measurement.

Impact estimation.

Automated joint-angle analysis.

Real-time technique feedback.

All future capabilities must remain explainable.

---

# Success Criteria

The Biomechanics model succeeds when:

Exercises are compared accurately.

Mechanical demands are explicit.

Transfer claims are justified.

Movement recommendations respect individual morphology.

Safety decisions become more coherent.

Athletes understand how movement produces performance.

---

# Final Principle

Biomechanics does not ask only:

What movement occurred?

It asks:

What forces produced it?

How were those forces transmitted?

What structures absorbed them?

And why does that matter for this athlete?